"""Concept notes, part C: ENGM, OS, PDS, TOC, GA."""
from content.notes_a import n, N  # noqa: F401

# ---------------- ENGM ----------------
n("ENGM", "Counting", "Basic counting: permutations n!/(n−r)!, combinations C(n,r) = n!/(r!(n−r)!), multiplication and addition principles, with repetition allowed: nʳ (ordered) or C(n+r−1, r) (unordered).",
  "Decide whether order matters and whether repetition is allowed, then pick the formula; split into disjoint cases when needed.",
  "Count objects, not arrangements, when items are identical (divide by repeat factorials).")
n("ENGM", "Pigeonhole & advanced counting", "Pigeonhole: n+1 objects in n boxes force a box with two objects; generalised: ⌈m/n⌉ in some box. Inclusion–exclusion and derangements D(n) = n!·Σ(−1)ᵏ/k! handle restrictions.",
  "Define the boxes, find the worst-case distribution, and add one. For forbidden positions use inclusion–exclusion.",
  "The pigeonhole bound is a guarantee, not an exact count.")
n("ENGM", "Recurrence relations", "A linear recurrence with constant coefficients is solved through its characteristic equation: distinct roots r give aₙ = Σ c·rⁿ; repeated roots add polynomial factors; non-homogeneous terms add a particular solution.",
  "Write the characteristic polynomial, find roots, fit constants from initial values, or simply iterate for small n.",
  "Check initial conditions; Fibonacci-type recurrences grow like φⁿ.")
n("ENGM", "Chromatic & connectivity", "Chromatic number χ(G) is the fewest colours for a proper colouring; χ ≥ clique number, bipartite ⇔ χ ≤ 2 ⇔ no odd cycle; Brooks: χ ≤ Δ except complete graphs and odd cycles. Connectivity needs a path between every pair.",
  "Find the largest clique for a lower bound and a valid colouring for an upper bound; a graph with Δ is (Δ+1)-colourable by greedy colouring.",
  "Planar graphs are 4-colourable; trees are 2-colourable.")
n("ENGM", "Degrees & counting", "Handshake lemma: Σ deg = 2|E|; a degree sequence is graphical if the Havel–Hakimi procedure succeeds; a tree has n−1 edges, a forest with k components n−k.",
  "Relate edges to the sum of degrees and use the formulas for special graphs (Kₙ, Kₘ,ₙ, cycles, trees).",
  "The number of odd-degree vertices is always even.")
n("ENGM", "Closure under operations", "A set is closed under an operation if applying it to members always returns a member; closure under the group operation is the first group axiom.",
  "Test the operation on arbitrary members or find a counterexample.",
  "Closure fails if a single pair leaves the set.")
n("ENGM", "Cyclic groups", "A cyclic group is generated by one element; in Zₙ the generators are the units k with gcd(k, n) = 1, so there are φ(n) of them; the order of kᵃ in a cyclic group of order n is n/gcd(a, n).",
  "Use gcd and Euler's φ to count generators and element orders; subgroups correspond to divisors of n.",
  "Every subgroup of a cyclic group is cyclic.")
n("ENGM", "Lagrange / group facts", "Lagrange: the order of a subgroup divides the order of the group; element orders divide |G|; a group of prime order is cyclic.",
  "List divisors of |G| for possible subgroup orders and test whether such subgroups can exist.",
  "The converse of Lagrange is false (A₄ has no subgroup of order 6).")
n("ENGM", "Permutation groups", "A permutation decomposes into disjoint cycles; its order is the lcm of the cycle lengths; a k-cycle is even iff k is odd; Sₙ has n! elements.",
  "Write the permutation in cycle notation, take the lcm for order, count transpositions (k−1 per k-cycle) for parity.",
  "Compose permutations right to left unless told otherwise.")
n("ENGM", "Continuity", "A function is continuous at a if lim x→a f(x) exists, f(a) is defined and the two are equal. Removable, jump and infinite discontinuities differ.",
  "Compare left and right limits with f(a); polynomials, exp, sin are continuous everywhere, rational functions except at zero denominators.",
  "Continuity does not imply differentiability (|x| at 0).")
n("ENGM", "Differentiation", "Derivative rules: power, product (uv)' = u'v + uv', quotient, chain rule; derivatives of eˣ, ln x, sin x, cos x. Differentiability implies continuity.",
  "Break the function into standard pieces and apply the rules, simplifying at the end.",
  "The chain rule multiplies by the derivative of the inside function.")
n("ENGM", "Limits", "Limits describe behaviour near a point: use factoring, rationalising, L'Hôpital's rule for 0/0 or ∞/∞, and standard limits such as sin x / x → 1 and (1 + 1/n)ⁿ → e.",
  "Substitute first; for an indeterminate form transform algebraically or differentiate numerator and denominator.",
  "L'Hôpital only applies to indeterminate quotients.")
n("ENGM", "Eigenvalue facts", "For an n×n matrix: Σ λ = trace, Π λ = det, eigenvalues of Aᵏ are λᵏ, A is singular iff 0 is an eigenvalue, triangular matrices have the diagonal as eigenvalues.",
  "Use trace and determinant relations instead of solving the characteristic polynomial when possible.",
  "Similar matrices share eigenvalues; eigenvectors of distinct eigenvalues are linearly independent.")
n("ENGM", "Eigenvalues", "Eigenvalues solve det(A − λI) = 0; for each λ the eigenvectors are the non-zero solutions of (A − λI)x = 0.",
  "Form the characteristic polynomial, find roots, and substitute to get eigenvectors.",
  "A 2×2 matrix has characteristic polynomial λ² − (trace)λ + det.")
n("ENGM", "Linear systems", "Ax = b is consistent iff rank(A) = rank([A|b]); unique solution if rank = number of unknowns, infinitely many if smaller, none if the ranks differ.",
  "Row-reduce the augmented matrix and compare ranks and unknowns.",
  "A homogeneous system always has the trivial solution.")
n("ENGM", "Determinant properties", "det(AB) = det A · det B; det(Aᵀ) = det A; swapping two rows changes the sign; scaling a row scales det; det(kA) = kⁿ det A; triangular det = product of diagonal.",
  "Apply the property directly instead of expanding.",
  "det(A + B) ≠ det A + det B.")
n("ENGM", "Determinants", "Compute a determinant by cofactor expansion along a row/column or by row reduction to triangular form (tracking row swaps and scalings).",
  "Expand along the row or column with most zeros, keeping the sign pattern (−1)^(i+j).",
  "Sign errors in cofactors are the usual mistake.")
n("ENGM", "Rank", "Rank is the number of linearly independent rows (or columns), equal to the number of non-zero rows after row reduction; rank(A) ≤ min(m, n).",
  "Reduce to row echelon form and count non-zero rows.",
  "Rank(AB) ≤ min(rank A, rank B).")
n("ENGM", "Rank-nullity", "Rank–nullity: rank(A) + nullity(A) = number of columns; the nullity is the dimension of the solution space of Ax = 0.",
  "Compute the rank, then nullity = columns − rank.",
  "Use columns (unknowns), not rows.")
n("ENGM", "Definite integrals", "Fundamental theorem: ∫ₐᵇ f = F(b) − F(a); use substitution, integration by parts and symmetry (odd functions integrate to 0 over symmetric limits).",
  "Find an antiderivative, evaluate at the limits, or use symmetry/standard integrals to shortcut.",
  "Change limits when substituting; improper integrals need limits.")
n("ENGM", "Maxima & minima", "Critical points satisfy f'(x) = 0; the second derivative test: f'' > 0 minimum, f'' < 0 maximum; check end points for global extrema on closed intervals.",
  "Differentiate, solve f' = 0, classify with f'', compare values at critical points and endpoints.",
  "f'' = 0 is inconclusive; local extrema need not be global.")
n("ENGM", "Divisor lattice", "The divisibility lattice on the divisors of n has meet = gcd and join = lcm; it is a Boolean algebra iff n is square-free; complements exist exactly in square-free cases.",
  "Factor n into primes, use gcd/lcm for meet/join, and count elements with ∏(eᵢ+1).",
  "Covering pairs differ by one prime.")
n("ENGM", "Lattice basics", "A lattice is a poset in which every pair has a least upper bound (join) and greatest lower bound (meet); distributive, complemented and bounded lattices have extra laws.",
  "Draw the Hasse diagram, find joins/meets of pairs, and test the property.",
  "A poset with a greatest and least element is not necessarily a lattice.")
n("ENGM", "Posets", "A partial order is reflexive, antisymmetric and transitive; a Hasse diagram draws only covering relations; maximal/minimal elements, chains and antichains describe its structure.",
  "Check the three properties and list maximal/minimal elements; for chains/antichains use Dilworth/Mirsky ideas.",
  "Maximal is not the same as greatest.")
n("ENGM", "Bayes & conditional", "Conditional probability P(A|B) = P(A∩B)/P(B); total probability P(B) = ΣP(B|Aᵢ)P(Aᵢ); Bayes' rule P(A|B) = P(B|A)P(A)/P(B).",
  "Draw a tree or table of the partitioned events, compute P(B) by total probability, then divide.",
  "P(A|B) and P(B|A) are different; base rates matter.")
n("ENGM", "Dice & coins", "For equally likely outcomes P(E) = favourable / total; for n coin tosses there are 2ⁿ outcomes, for two dice 36; use symmetry and complements.",
  "List or count outcomes systematically (tables for dice sums) and divide.",
  "Distinguish ordered from unordered outcomes.")
n("ENGM", "Independence", "Events A, B are independent iff P(A∩B) = P(A)P(B); mutually exclusive events with positive probability are never independent.",
  "Compute the joint probability and compare with the product.",
  "Pairwise independence does not imply mutual independence.")
n("ENGM", "Boolean functions count", "There are 2^(2ⁿ) Boolean functions of n variables; a function is determined by its truth table.",
  "Count truth-table rows (2ⁿ) and raise 2 to that power.",
  "Self-dual and monotone function counts need additional constraints.")
n("ENGM", "Counting models", "Count satisfying assignments of a propositional formula by truth table or by splitting: an implication p→q is false only when p is true and q false; a conjunction of k independent clauses multiplies counts.",
  "Enumerate the 2ⁿ assignments or count falsifying ones and subtract from 2ⁿ.",
  "Check variables that do not appear in the formula (they double the count).")
n("ENGM", "Equivalences", "Key equivalences: p→q ≡ ¬p ∨ q ≡ ¬q → ¬p; De Morgan; distributivity; p↔q ≡ (p→q)∧(q→p). The converse and inverse are not equivalent to p→q.",
  "Rewrite both formulas using implication elimination and compare, or use a truth table.",
  "The converse (q→p) is not equivalent to the original implication.")
n("ENGM", "First-order logic", "Quantifiers: ∀x P(x) holds for every element, ∃x P(x) for some element; negation flips them: ¬∀x P ≡ ∃x ¬P. Order matters: ∀x∃y differs from ∃y∀x.",
  "Translate to English, check on the given domain, and push negations inward.",
  "∀x∃y R(x,y) does not imply ∃y∀x R(x,y).")
n("ENGM", "Satisfiability", "A formula is satisfiable if some assignment makes it true, a tautology if all do, a contradiction if none; SAT is NP-complete in general, 2-SAT is polynomial.",
  "Search for a satisfying assignment or show a contradiction by unit propagation.",
  "A tautology is satisfiable; a satisfiable formula is not necessarily a tautology.")
n("ENGM", "Distributions", "Standard distributions: Binomial(n,p) mean np, variance np(1−p); Poisson(λ) mean λ, variance λ; Uniform, Normal(μ,σ²); exponential mean 1/λ.",
  "Identify the distribution from the experiment and apply its formula for probabilities, mean and variance.",
  "Poisson approximates Binomial for large n and small p.")
n("ENGM", "Expectation & variance", "E[X] = Σ x·P(x); linearity E[aX+bY] = aE[X] + bE[Y]; Var(X) = E[X²] − (E[X])²; Var(aX+b) = a²Var(X); independent variances add.",
  "Compute the needed moments from the distribution and substitute into the formulas.",
  "Linearity of expectation holds without independence; variance additivity needs it.")
n("ENGM", "Closures", "The reflexive, symmetric and transitive closures add the minimum pairs needed to obtain the property; the transitive closure uses reachability (Warshall).",
  "Add (a,a) for reflexive, (b,a) for each (a,b) for symmetric, and repeatedly (a,c) for (a,b),(b,c) for transitive.",
  "Closing in a different order can give different results (symmetric then transitive includes reflexive pairs).")
n("ENGM", "Counting relations & functions", "On sets with |A| = m, |B| = n: relations 2^(mn), functions nᵐ, injective functions n!/(n−m)!, bijections n! (when m = n), subsets 2ⁿ.",
  "Choose the right object (relation vs function) and count choices per element.",
  "A relation allows any subset of A×B; a function needs exactly one image per element.")
n("ENGM", "Divisibility counting", "Count multiples with ⌊N/k⌋; numbers divisible by a or b: ⌊N/a⌋ + ⌊N/b⌋ − ⌊N/lcm(a,b)⌋ (inclusion–exclusion).",
  "Apply inclusion–exclusion with lcms of the divisors.",
  "Use lcm, not product, when the divisors are not coprime.")
n("ENGM", "Inclusion–exclusion", "|A ∪ B| = |A| + |B| − |A ∩ B|; for three sets add back the triple intersection: |A∪B∪C| = Σ|A| − Σ|A∩B| + |A∩B∩C|.",
  "List single, pairwise and triple intersections and alternate signs.",
  "Forgetting the triple-intersection term is the usual error.")
n("ENGM", "Relation properties", "A relation is reflexive (all (a,a)), symmetric ((a,b) ⇒ (b,a)), antisymmetric, transitive; equivalence = reflexive + symmetric + transitive, partial order = reflexive + antisymmetric + transitive.",
  "Test each property directly on the pairs; one counterexample refutes a property.",
  "Antisymmetric is not the opposite of symmetric.")
for t in ("Combinatorics & Recurrences", "Graph Theory", "Groups & Monoids", "Limits, Continuity & Differentiation", "Linear Equations & Eigenvalues", "Matrices & Determinants",
          "Maxima, Minima & Integration", "Partial Orders & Lattices", "Probability Basics & Bayes", "Propositional & First-Order Logic", "Random Variables & Distributions", "Sets, Relations & Functions"):
    n("ENGM", "Concepts", "Engineering mathematics for GATE rests on definitions, standard formulas and quick hand verification on small cases.",
      "Recall the defining property, then test it on a small example to confirm.", "Check the conditions of a theorem before using it.", topic=t)

# ---------------- OS ----------------
n("OS", "FCFS", "FCFS runs processes in arrival order, non-preemptively; waiting time = start − arrival, turnaround = completion − arrival; the convoy effect hurts short jobs behind long ones.",
  "Order by arrival, compute start and completion times in sequence, subtract.",
  "Idle gaps appear if the next process arrives after the CPU is free.")
n("OS", "Round Robin", "Round Robin gives each process a time quantum in turn, preempting it if unfinished; a new arrival joins the ready queue tail; response time is bounded by (n−1)·q.",
  "Simulate the ready queue with a Gantt chart, adding arrivals before re-queuing the preempted process (tie rule as stated).",
  "Context-switch time is usually ignored unless given; a huge quantum behaves like FCFS.")
n("OS", "SJF (non-preemptive)", "Shortest-job-first picks the ready process with the smallest burst; it minimises average waiting time among non-preemptive algorithms but can starve long jobs.",
  "At each completion choose the shortest burst among arrived processes.",
  "Only processes that have already arrived are candidates.")
n("OS", "SRTF (preemptive SJF)", "SRTF preempts the running process whenever a new arrival has a shorter remaining time; it gives the minimum average waiting time overall.",
  "Re-evaluate remaining times at every arrival and build the Gantt chart; waiting = turnaround − burst.",
  "Compare with the remaining time of the running process, not its original burst.")
n("OS", "Banker's algorithm", "Banker's algorithm keeps the system in a safe state: Need = Max − Allocation; a state is safe if some order lets every process finish using Available plus released resources.",
  "Find a process whose Need ≤ Available, let it finish (Available += Allocation), repeat; if all finish the state is safe.",
  "A request is granted only if it keeps the state safe; unsafe does not necessarily mean deadlock.")
n("OS", "Deadlock-free resource count", "With n processes each needing up to k units of one resource type, deadlock is impossible if the total R ≥ n(k−1) + 1.",
  "Compute the worst case where every process holds k−1 units, then add one.",
  "The condition is sufficient; deadlock-free for fewer resources is possible only with other information.")
n("OS", "Disk access time", "Disk access time = seek time + rotational latency (average half a rotation) + transfer time; transfer = block size / transfer rate.",
  "Compute each component in the same units and add; rotation time = 60/RPM seconds.",
  "Average rotational latency is half a revolution.")
n("OS", "Disk scheduling", "FCFS serves requests in order; SSTF takes the nearest; SCAN (elevator) sweeps to one end and reverses; C-SCAN returns to the start and sweeps again; LOOK/C-LOOK turn at the last request.",
  "Order the requests as the algorithm dictates and add the absolute head movements.",
  "SCAN goes to the physical end; LOOK stops at the last request.")
n("OS", "Inodes", "An inode holds file metadata and block pointers: direct, single-, double- and triple-indirect; maximum file size = (direct + P + P² + P³) × block size, with P = block size / pointer size.",
  "Compute pointers per indirect block, then sum the data blocks reachable by each pointer level.",
  "Remember the pointer size and that metadata is not file data.")
n("OS", "Contiguous allocation", "Contiguous allocation uses first-fit, best-fit or worst-fit on free holes; it causes external fragmentation, which compaction can fix.",
  "Walk through the free list for each request according to the policy.",
  "Best-fit leaves tiny unusable holes; worst-fit leaves large remainders.")
n("OS", "Demand paging", "Demand paging loads pages on first use; effective access time EAT = (1−p)·memory time + p·(fault service time), with fault rate p.",
  "Plug p and the access times into EAT; solve for p if EAT is bounded.",
  "Fault service time is millions of nanoseconds, so p must be tiny.")
n("OS", "Fragmentation", "Internal fragmentation wastes space inside allocated units (paging's last page); external fragmentation is free memory split into unusable holes (contiguous allocation).",
  "Compute the waste from rounding up to the unit size or from unusable holes.",
  "Paging has internal but no external fragmentation.")
n("OS", "Multi-level paging", "A multi-level page table splits the page number into indices per level, each level fitting in one page; the number of levels L and entries per page determine the address split.",
  "Compute entries per page (page size / entry size), bits per level, then levels = ⌈page-number bits / bits per level⌉.",
  "Each extra level adds one memory access on a TLB miss.")
n("OS", "Page replacement", "FIFO evicts the oldest page, LRU the least recently used, Optimal the page used furthest in the future; FIFO can show Belady's anomaly (more frames, more faults), LRU and Optimal cannot.",
  "Simulate the reference string with the frame set, counting a fault whenever the page is absent.",
  "Count faults on initial loads too; keep recency or arrival order exactly.")
n("OS", "Paging arithmetic", "Logical address = page number | offset; page size = 2^offset bits; page-table size = number of pages × entry size; physical address = frame number × page size + offset.",
  "Split the address bits, count pages and entries, and compute table sizes.",
  "Use the page size given in bytes consistently.")
n("OS", "TLB / EAT", "EAT with a TLB = h·(t_tlb + t_mem) + (1−h)·(t_tlb + 2·t_mem) for a one-level page table; hit ratio h is the key parameter.",
  "Weight the hit and miss paths by their probabilities and add memory accesses (page-table walk + data).",
  "On a miss multi-level tables add one access per level.")
n("OS", "Overheads", "Process creation and context switches cost more than thread switches because threads share an address space; user-level threads switch without the kernel but block together.",
  "Compare the overhead components (save/restore registers, TLB flush, kernel entry) for the mechanisms.",
  "Kernel threads can run on several CPUs; user-level threads cannot.")
n("OS", "fork()", "fork() creates a child process that continues after the call; n sequential forks produce 2ⁿ processes in total (including the parent), 2ⁿ − 1 children.",
  "Trace the process tree; return value 0 in the child and the child's PID in the parent decides later branches.",
  "Output buffers copied across fork can duplicate printed text.")
n("OS", "fork() with conditions", "Each fork() creates one new process that continues from the same point; conditions with && or || use short-circuit evaluation so some forks are skipped.",
  "Track which processes execute each fork() (parent returns non-zero, child returns 0) and count the processes created.",
  "In A && fork() the fork only runs when A is true; in A || fork() only when A is false.")
n("OS", "Semaphore counting", "A counting semaphore starts at a value; wait (P) decrements and blocks at 0, signal (V) increments or wakes a waiter; the final value = initial + signals − waits that completed.",
  "Count the completed P and V operations over all processes, taking blocking into account.",
  "A blocked P does not decrement until it is released.")
n("OS", "Semaphores", "Semaphores enforce mutual exclusion (binary semaphore initialised to 1) and ordering (initial 0); misuse leads to deadlock or lost wake-ups.",
  "Trace the semaphore values through the interleavings that the question allows.",
  "Check for circular waits among multiple semaphores.")
for t in ("CPU Scheduling", "Deadlocks", "File Systems & Disk Scheduling", "Memory Management & Paging", "Processes, Threads & Syscalls", "Synchronization"):
    n("OS", "Concepts", "The operating system manages the CPU, memory, files and devices on behalf of processes and enforces protection and synchronisation.",
      "Recall the definition of the mechanism and the metric it optimises.", "Terms such as process vs thread or deadlock vs starvation are easy to swap.", topic=t)

# ---------------- PDS ----------------
n("PDS", "Expression evaluation", "Stack-based evaluation of postfix expressions pushes operands and applies each operator to the top two values (second popped is the left operand); infix-to-postfix conversion uses operator precedence and a stack.",
  "Scan left to right, push numbers, apply operators on pop, and watch operand order for − and /.",
  "Right associativity of ^ and the order of popped operands are the typical slips.")
n("PDS", "Queues", "A queue is FIFO; a circular array queue wraps indices with modulo size, and with front/rear pointers it holds at most size − 1 elements to tell full from empty (or uses a count).",
  "Simulate enqueue/dequeue updates of front and rear modulo n.",
  "Empty and full conditions look identical without the extra slot or counter.")
n("PDS", "Stack permutations", "Which output orders a stack can produce from an input sequence: a permutation is possible iff no pattern 'c, a, b' (a<b<c) appears; the number of valid outputs for n inputs is the Catalan number.",
  "Simulate pushes and pops to see if the target sequence can be produced.",
  "Pushing in input order only; popping order is the output.")
n("PDS", "Loops & control flow", "Evaluate loops by tracing variables per iteration, including the exit condition and any break/continue; compound assignments and pre/post-increment change the order of effects.",
  "Make a small table of variable values per iteration.",
  "Check whether the condition is tested before or after the body.")
n("PDS", "Macros & scope", "C macros are textual substitutions without type checking or extra parentheses; static variables retain value between calls; block scope shadows outer variables.",
  "Expand the macro literally and apply operator precedence to the expanded text.",
  "#define SQ(x) x*x with SQ(a+1) expands to a+1*a+1.")
n("PDS", "Operators & arithmetic", "C evaluates operators by precedence and associativity; integer division truncates toward zero; % has the sign of the dividend; i++ yields the old value and ++i the new; evaluation order of operands is unspecified.",
  "Apply precedence, evaluate sub-expressions carefully, and keep integer types.",
  "Integer overflow and char/int promotions are common traps.")
n("PDS", "Pointers & arrays", "In C an array name is the address of its first element; p[i] equals *(p + i); pointer arithmetic scales by the element size; strings end with '\\0'.",
  "Track addresses as base + index × size, and dereference accordingly.",
  "sizeof on a pointer parameter gives the pointer size, not the array size.")
n("PDS", "Structs & memory", "Struct layout follows member order with alignment padding: each member is placed at a multiple of its alignment and the total size is a multiple of the largest alignment.",
  "Lay out members sequentially adding padding, then round the total up.",
  "Reordering members can reduce size; unions share storage for their largest member.")
n("PDS", "Representation costs", "Adjacency matrix: O(V²) space, O(1) edge test; adjacency list: O(V + E) space, O(deg) edge test; traversals cost O(V²) vs O(V + E).",
  "Count space and operation cost for the given graph size.",
  "For sparse graphs lists win; for dense graphs matrices are competitive.")
n("PDS", "Traversals", "BFS uses a queue and visits by increasing distance; DFS uses a stack/recursion and goes deep first; tree traversal orders differ by when the node is visited.",
  "Simulate the algorithm with the stated tie-breaking (usually increasing vertex order).",
  "DFS and BFS orders depend on adjacency order.")
n("PDS", "Binary heaps", "A binary heap is a complete tree in an array; insert and delete cost O(log n); buildheap is O(n); heap sort is O(n log n) and in place.",
  "Apply sift-up/down operations explicitly to the array.",
  "The heap array is not sorted; only parent–child order is guaranteed.")
n("PDS", "Hash tables", "Hash tables place key k at h(k) mod m; collisions use chaining or probing; load factor α = n/m controls performance.",
  "Insert in order, probing as needed, and note final positions.",
  "Different probing schemes (linear vs quadratic) give different layouts.")
n("PDS", "d-ary heaps & indices", "In a d-ary heap the children of index i (1-based) are d(i−1)+2 … d·i+1 and the parent is ⌊(i−2)/d⌋ + 1; height ≈ log_d n.",
  "Plug into the index formulas, checking the base index.",
  "0-based and 1-based formulas differ.")
n("PDS", "Cycle detection", "Floyd's tortoise-and-hare detects a cycle with two pointers moving at speeds 1 and 2 in O(n) time and O(1) space; the pointers meet iff a cycle exists.",
  "Advance slow by 1 and fast by 2; they meet inside the cycle; reset one to the head to find the cycle start.",
  "The meeting point is not necessarily the cycle start.")
n("PDS", "List operations", "Linked-list operations rewire next pointers: insertion/deletion at a known node is O(1) but searching is O(n); reversing needs three pointers (prev, curr, next).",
  "Draw the list and apply the pointer updates in order.",
  "Update pointers in the right order or the rest of the list is lost.")
n("PDS", "Recursion traces", "Each recursive call has its own frame; the trace of calls and returns shows the order of side effects; depth equals the longest chain of pending calls.",
  "Expand calls in order, computing returns bottom-up.",
  "Print statements before and after the recursive call give opposite orders.")
n("PDS", "Recursive functions", "Evaluate recursive functions by expanding the definition to the base case; many have closed forms (Collatz steps, Fibonacci, GCD by Euclid).",
  "Compute small cases by hand and look for the pattern.",
  "Check the base case and whether the recursion reduces its argument.")
n("PDS", "AVL trees", "An AVL tree keeps balance factor |h_left − h_right| ≤ 1 through rotations (LL, RR, LR, RL); minimum nodes N(h) = N(h−1) + N(h−2) + 1.",
  "Insert in order, check balance factors along the path, and rotate at the first unbalanced node from the inserted leaf.",
  "Deletion can need rotations all the way up.")
n("PDS", "BST", "A binary search tree keeps left < node < right; inorder traversal gives sorted order; search/insert are O(h), worst case O(n) for sorted insertions.",
  "Insert keys in the given order and read the shape or traversal.",
  "The shape depends on insertion order; the number of distinct BSTs on n keys is the Catalan number.")
n("PDS", "Tree properties", "A binary tree with n nodes has n − 1 edges and height ≥ ⌊log₂ n⌋; full binary tree: leaves = internal + 1; maximum nodes at level i is 2ⁱ.",
  "Relate leaves, internal nodes and degrees through edge counts.",
  "Distinguish height (edges) from number of levels.")
n("PDS", "Tree traversals", "Preorder visits node, left, right; inorder left, node, right; postorder left, right, node; a tree is uniquely determined by inorder plus preorder or postorder.",
  "Use the root from preorder/postorder and split inorder to rebuild or read traversals.",
  "Preorder and postorder alone do not determine a tree uniquely.")
