# ---------------- TOC ----------------
n("TOC", "Closure properties", "Regular languages are closed under union, intersection, complement, concatenation, star, reversal and homomorphism; CFLs are closed under union, concatenation, star and reversal but not intersection or complement; decidable languages are closed under complement, RE languages are not.",
  "Look up the language class and operation in the closure table; if absent, build a counterexample (e.g. aⁿbⁿcᵐ ∩ aᵐbⁿcⁿ).",
  "Intersection of a CFL with a regular language is context-free.")
n("TOC", "Language classification", "Classify a language by the machine that recognises it: finite memory → regular; one stack → context-free; bounded tape → context-sensitive; unbounded tape → RE. Use pumping lemmas to show a language is not regular/CF.",
  "Check whether a finite automaton can track the required counts; if comparison of unbounded counts is needed it is not regular.",
  "aⁿbⁿ is context-free but not regular; aⁿbⁿcⁿ is not context-free.")
n("TOC", "Counting strings", "Count strings of a given length in a language by dynamic programming over the automaton/grammar or by combinatorial formulas.",
  "Define states and transitions and count paths of the required length.",
  "Overlapping cases must be counted once.")
n("TOC", "Grammars", "A context-free grammar generates strings by replacing non-terminals; Chomsky normal form has productions A → BC or A → a, and a derivation of a string of length n in CNF takes 2n − 1 steps.",
  "Apply productions in sequence and track the generated string; convert by eliminating ε, unit and long productions.",
  "ε-productions complicate CNF conversion.")
n("TOC", "Pushdown automata", "A PDA is a finite automaton with a stack; DPDAs are strictly weaker than NPDAs; acceptance is by final state or by empty stack (equivalent in power for NPDAs).",
  "Trace stack contents for an input and see whether the machine can finish in an accepting configuration.",
  "Deterministic CFLs are closed under complement but not union.")
n("TOC", "Counting accepted strings", "Count the strings of length k accepted by a DFA by counting paths from the start to an accepting state of length k: multiply the transition-count matrix.",
  "Compute the vector of path counts per state step by step.",
  "Different paths of the same string do not exist in a DFA (one path per string).")
n("TOC", "Minimal DFA", "The minimal DFA for a language has one state per Myhill–Nerode equivalence class; merge indistinguishable states by table filling after removing unreachable states.",
  "Remove unreachable states, split accepting from non-accepting, refine partitions by transitions until stable.",
  "A DFA is complete; the dead state counts as a state.")
n("TOC", "Regular expressions", "Regular expressions use union, concatenation and star; equivalent to finite automata. Simplify with identities such as (a + b)* = (a*b*)* and ε + a·a* = a*.",
  "Describe the language in words and compare strings of small length with the candidate expressions.",
  "(a+b)* is all strings; a*b* is a before b only.")
n("TOC", "Subset construction", "Subset construction converts an NFA with n states into a DFA with at most 2ⁿ states by tracking the set of NFA states reached; ε-closures are included.",
  "Start from the ε-closure of the start state and compute transitions for each symbol for new subsets.",
  "Only reachable subsets need to be built.")
n("TOC", "Complexity of automata", "Standard sizes: complement of a DFA keeps n states, product of two DFAs has n·m states, NFA → DFA can need 2ⁿ states, reversal of a DFA may need 2ⁿ states.",
  "Recall the construction and its blow-up and compare with the options.",
  "NFA complement is exponential in general.")
n("TOC", "Decidability", "Halting is undecidable; by Rice's theorem every non-trivial semantic property of RE languages is undecidable; emptiness and equivalence are decidable for regular and (emptiness only) context-free languages.",
  "Reduce the problem to halting (or recall the table of decidable problems).",
  "RE ≠ decidable: RE languages are semi-decidable only.")
for t in ("Closure & Pumping Lemma", "Context-Free Languages & PDA", "Regular Languages & Finite Automata", "Turing Machines & Decidability"):
    n("TOC", "Concepts", "The Chomsky hierarchy orders languages by the power of the machine needed: finite automata ⊂ pushdown automata ⊂ linear-bounded automata ⊂ Turing machines.",
      "Place the language or machine in the hierarchy and use its closure/decidability properties.", "Equality of classes is rarely true; check strict inclusions.", topic=t)

# ---------------- GA ----------------
n("GA", "Percentages", "Percent means per hundred: x% of y = xy/100; successive changes multiply (+a% then −b% gives (1 + a/100)(1 − b/100) − 1); percent change = change / original × 100.",
  "Convert percentages to multipliers (1.20, 0.85) and chain them.", "A 20% rise followed by a 20% fall is a 4% net fall, not zero.")
n("GA", "Profit & loss", "Profit = SP − CP; profit % = profit/CP × 100; marked price and discount: SP = MP(1 − d); successive discounts multiply.",
  "Work with CP = 100 or a symbol, derive SP, and compare.", "Profit % is on cost price unless stated; discount is on marked price.")
n("GA", "Time & work", "If A does a job in a days, his rate is 1/a per day; rates add when people work together; time = 1 / combined rate.",
  "Add rates, invert for time, handle partial days by fractions of work.", "Work done is rate × time; pipes and cisterns are the same idea with signs.")
n("GA", "Time, speed, distance", "Distance = speed × time; average speed = total distance / total time (harmonic for equal distances); relative speed adds (opposite) or subtracts (same direction).",
  "Convert units (km/h to m/s multiply by 5/18) and set up one equation per segment.", "Average speed is not the arithmetic mean of speeds over equal distances.")
n("GA", "Interest", "Simple interest SI = PRT/100; compound amount A = P(1 + R/100)ᵀ; the difference between CI and SI for two years is P(R/100)².",
  "Choose SI or CI and compute year by year if compounding is more frequent.", "Compound interest compounds on interest as well.")
n("GA", "Ratio & mixtures", "Ratios compare quantities; proportion a/b = c/d; alligation finds the mixing ratio from the means: (higher − mean) : (mean − lower).",
  "Convert ratios to a common unit and use the alligation cross.", "Mix ratios by quantity, not by price.")
n("GA", "Averages & ages", "Average = sum / count; adding or removing an element changes the sum, not the average directly; age problems use linear equations over time.",
  "Express sums via the average and set up the equation for the change.", "Keep track of the time shift (x years ago, y years later).")
n("GA", "Algebra", "Solve linear and quadratic equations by factoring, substitution or the quadratic formula; check solutions against the original conditions.",
  "Translate words to equations, simplify and check.", "Reject extraneous roots.")
n("GA", "Number properties", "Divisibility tests, HCF/LCM, primes and remainders: HCF × LCM = product of two numbers; remainders follow modular arithmetic.",
  "Prime-factorise and use the rules for gcd and lcm.", "The last-digit cycle of powers repeats with period 4 (or 2).")
n("GA", "Geometry & mensuration", "Areas and volumes: circle πr², sphere 4/3 πr³, cylinder πr²h, cone 1/3 πr²h; Pythagoras in right triangles; similar figures scale areas by the square of the ratio.",
  "Draw the figure, label lengths and apply the formula.", "Use consistent units; check whether the question asks for area, perimeter or volume.")
n("GA", "Clocks & calendars", "In 12 hours the minute and hour hands coincide 11 times; hands are 6° and 0.5° apart per minute; a leap year has 366 days; an odd-day count gives the weekday.",
  "Use the relative speed 5.5° per minute or day arithmetic mod 7.", "Century years are leap only if divisible by 400.")
n("GA", "Counting & probability (GA)", "Counting uses multiplication and addition principles, permutations and combinations; probability = favourable / total for equally likely outcomes.",
  "Count outcomes systematically and divide.", "Check ordered vs unordered and repetition.")
n("GA", "Number series", "Find the rule linking terms: constant or growing differences, ratios, squares/cubes, alternating or interleaved patterns.",
  "Write differences, ratios and second differences until a pattern appears.", "Test the rule on every given term, not just two.")
n("GA", "Venn & sets", "Venn diagrams count overlapping groups: n(A∪B) = n(A) + n(B) − n(A∩B); fill the innermost region first and work outward.",
  "Draw the diagram, fill the intersection, derive the rest by subtraction.", "Total must include the 'none' region.")
n("GA", "Directions & distances", "Direction problems use coordinates: right-angle turns and distances combine through Pythagoras to give displacement.",
  "Place the start at the origin and track coordinates after each move.", "Left/right depend on the current facing.")
n("GA", "Coding & ordering", "Coding problems apply a consistent rule to letters or numbers (shifts, reversal, position values); ordering problems rank items using the given comparisons.",
  "Find the rule using the examples, then apply it; build the order from the clues.", "Check the rule against every example.")
n("GA", "Cubes & counting figures", "A cube of side n painted outside has (n−2)³ unpainted cubes, 6(n−2)² single-face, 12(n−2) two-face and 8 three-face cubes.",
  "Apply the counts per type of cube and verify they sum to n³.", "For n = 2 there are no inner cubes.")
