n("TOC", "Ambiguity & parse trees", "A grammar is ambiguous if some string has two distinct parse trees (equivalently two leftmost derivations); the language may still be unambiguous, but inherently ambiguous context-free languages also exist.",
  "Look for a string with two derivation trees (for example from a rule like E → E + E | E * E | id); remove ambiguity by layering the grammar with precedence and associativity.",
  "Ambiguity belongs to the grammar, not the language, and deciding ambiguity is undecidable in general.")
